+### Camera Ray Sampling for Translucent Materials
+Samples camera rays that reach translucent material after diffuse interactions with a small number of samples, as low as one per ray [S14].
+**Steps:**
+1.  Compute the exit point for the camera ray to determine the maximum depth along the ray, denoted as $d_{\max}$ [S14].
+2.  Place exactly $k_{\max}$ samples along the ray using the derived depth formula [S14].
+**Formula:**
+$$ d_k = -\frac{1}{\sigma_t} \log \left( 1 - \frac{|k(1 - e^{-\sigma_t d_{\max}})|}{k_{\max}} \right) $$
+where $\sigma_t$ is the extinction coefficient, $k$ is the sample index, and $k_{\max}$ is the total number of samples [S14].
+**Parameters:**
+*   $k_{\max}$: The total number of sample points to place along the ray. The method ensures exactly $k_{\max}$ samples are placed [S14].
+**Pitfall:**
+*   **Small Object Width:** The sampling scheme handles small object widths by ensuring $k_{\max}$ samples are always placed, preventing undersampling [S14].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot n_{pts\_per\_ray})$ for generating sample points, where $N$ is the number of rays [S6, S10]. The computation is highly parallelizable on GPUs [S4, S10].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot n_{pts\_per\_ray} \cdot 3)$ for storing sample points and $O(N \cdot n_{pts\_per\_ray})$ for depths/lengths [S6, S10].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot n_{pts\_per\_ray})$ for generating sample points, where $N$ is the number of rays [S6, unofficial, S10, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot n_{pts\_per\_ray} \cdot 3)$ for storing sample points and $O(N \cdot n_{pts\_per\_ray})$ for depths/lengths [S6, unofficial, S10, unofficial].
 *   **Performance:** Uniform sampling is the easiest to implement but has the lowest efficiency in most cases compared to spatial skipping or PDF-based methods [S1]. It wastes samples in empty space and misses high-density regions [S1].
 *   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, deterministic (without perturbation), low overhead [S1].
